{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Please, Adonai, I am not a man of words.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: for seven days the Holy One, blessed be He, was persuading Moses to go to Egypt — from yesterday and from the day before yesterday are three, and three added from the plural form are six, and he was standing on the seventh day. And as for what he said, ״for I am heavy of mouth,״ they said in the Midrash: Moses grew up in the palace of Pharaoh, and he would take Pharaoh's crown and cast it to the ground. The king wanted to condemn him to death, and they said to him: the astrologers — this is the one we told you about, who is destined to deliver Israel and destroy your kingdom. Pharaoh took counsel with his wise men, and included among his advisers were Jethro, Job, and Balaam. One said: he is liable to death; and another said: he is a child and has no understanding. What did they do? They brought him a plate; they placed in it a piece of gold and a live coal. They said: if he takes the gold, it is known that he is of understanding and liable to death; and if he takes the coal, it is known that he has no understanding and is exempt from death. He wanted to take the gold, and an angel came and inclined his hand, and he took the coal and put it into his mouth, and he became ערל שפתים — of uncircumcised lips." "<b>And according to</b> this Midrash, he says: ״heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue am I from my youth״ — how much more so now that I am old, and You have not healed me from the time You spoke to Your servant and commanded me with this mission. And the reason the Holy One, blessed be He, did not heal him and did not remove the heaviness of his mouth is because he did not pray; and the reason he did not pray is because he did not want to go." "<b>And</b> our Rabbi Hananel wrote: the fact that he mentioned two things, ״heavy of mouth״ and ״heavy of tongue,״ indicates that Moses our master was not fluent in pronunciation of the dental/sibilant letters ז ש ר ס ״ץ, which are the letters of the teeth — this is what he meant by ״for I am heavy of mouth.״ Nor was he fluent in the letters of the tongue, which are the letters ד ט ל נ ״ת, and concerning this he said ״and heavy of tongue.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "The last clause reads הש\"י, resolved here as a divine epithet (הש\"י = השם יתברך); alternatively it could be a corruption of הקב\"ה."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:08.809946352Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}